# Same rule proxy/.gitattributes already carries, and for the same reason:
# a run manifest publishes sha256 over delivered bytes, so core.autocrlf must
# not be able to change them. This landed after `canary.py freeze-audit --json
# > file` produced a CRLF artefact on Windows (Python's text-mode stdout
# translates on the way out) whose committed blob then hashed differently from
# the working copy the manifest was built against -- a manifest that cannot be
# reproduced from a fresh checkout is a manifest you have to take on trust.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
